Tweets from one user are naturally ordered because each new tweet is placed at the front of a linked list. The feed is a merge of these lists. A priority queue keeps the newest current tweet from each visible user.

After taking one tweet, the next older tweet from the same list is added. Since only 10 results are needed, the heap never has to process a long part of every list.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Twitter {
    private static class Tweet {
        int id;
        int time;
        Tweet next;

        Tweet(int id, int time, Tweet next) {
            this.id = id;
            this.time = time;
            this.next = next;
        }
    }

    private final Map<Integer, Tweet> latestTweet;
    private final Map<Integer, Set<Integer>> following;
    private int time;

    public Twitter() {
        latestTweet = new HashMap<>();
        following = new HashMap<>();
        time = 0;
    }

    public void postTweet(int userId, int tweetId) {
        Tweet oldHead = latestTweet.get(userId);
        latestTweet.put(userId, new Tweet(tweetId, time++, oldHead));
    }

    public List<Integer> getNewsFeed(int userId) {
        List<Integer> feed = new ArrayList<>();
        PriorityQueue<Tweet> heap = new PriorityQueue<>(
            (a, b) -> Integer.compare(b.time, a.time)
        );

        heap.offer(latestTweet.get(userId));

        Set<Integer> users = following.getOrDefault(userId, Collections.emptySet());
        for (int followeeId : users) {
            Tweet head = latestTweet.get(followeeId);
            if (head != null) {
                heap.offer(head);
            }
        }

        while (!heap.isEmpty() && feed.size() < 10) {
            Tweet current = heap.poll();
            if (current == null) {
                continue;
            }

            feed.add(current.id);
            if (current.next != null) {
                heap.offer(current.next);
            }
        }

        return feed;
    }

    public void follow(int followerId, int followeeId) {
        if (followerId == followeeId) {
            return;
        }
        following.computeIfAbsent(followerId, key -> new HashSet<>()).add(followeeId);
    }

    public void unfollow(int followerId, int followeeId) {
        if (followerId == followeeId) {
            return;
        }
        Set<Integer> users = following.get(followerId);
        if (users != null) {
            users.remove(followeeId);
        }
    }
}
```

The null check for the user's own tweet matters when a user asks for a feed before posting anything. For a small example, if user 1 has tweets 5 then 3, the list is `3 -> 5`. The heap starts with 3, and after removing it, 5 becomes the next candidate.

Let K be the user plus the number of followees. Posting and follow changes are O(1) average. Building the heap takes O(K log K), and extracting 10 tweets takes O(10 log K). The stored lists and relationships use O(T + F) space, with O(K) temporary heap space.

A timestamp gives every post a clear order across different users. The code gathers the current user's tweets and the tweets of every followed user into a max-heap. Removing from that heap returns the newest tweet first, so the first 10 removed IDs form the feed.

The follow map uses `HashSet<int>`, which also prevents the same follow relationship from being stored twice. Self-follow and self-unfollow are ignored.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

class Twitter
{
    private class Tweet
    {
        public int Time;
        public int Id;

        public Tweet(int time, int id)
        {
            Time = time;
            Id = id;
        }
    }

    private readonly Dictionary<int, List<Tweet>> tweets;
    private readonly Dictionary<int, HashSet<int>> following;
    private int time;

    public Twitter()
    {
        tweets = new Dictionary<int, List<Tweet>>();
        following = new Dictionary<int, HashSet<int>>();
        time = 0;
    }

    public void PostTweet(int userId, int tweetId)
    {
        if (!tweets.ContainsKey(userId))
        {
            tweets[userId] = new List<Tweet>();
        }

        tweets[userId].Add(new Tweet(time, tweetId));
        time++;
    }

    public List<int> GetNewsFeed(int userId)
    {
        var heap = new PriorityQueue<Tweet, int>();
        AddTweets(userId, heap);

        if (following.ContainsKey(userId))
        {
            foreach (int followeeId in following[userId])
            {
                AddTweets(followeeId, heap);
            }
        }

        var feed = new List<int>();
        while (heap.Count > 0 && feed.Count < 10)
        {
            Tweet current = heap.Dequeue();
            feed.Add(current.Id);
        }

        return feed;
    }

    public void Follow(int followerId, int followeeId)
    {
        if (followerId == followeeId)
        {
            return;
        }

        if (!following.ContainsKey(followerId))
        {
            following[followerId] = new HashSet<int>();
        }
        following[followerId].Add(followeeId);
    }

    public void Unfollow(int followerId, int followeeId)
    {
        if (followerId == followeeId)
        {
            return;
        }

        if (following.ContainsKey(followerId))
        {
            following[followerId].Remove(followeeId);
        }
    }

    private void AddTweets(int userId, PriorityQueue<Tweet, int> heap)
    {
        if (!tweets.ContainsKey(userId))
        {
            return;
        }

        foreach (Tweet tweet in tweets[userId])
        {
            heap.Enqueue(tweet, -tweet.Time);
        }
    }
}
```

The negative priority makes a larger timestamp come out first. If nobody has posted, `AddTweets` does nothing and the returned list stays empty. The code keeps the stored tweet lists unchanged.

For N visible tweets, feed creation takes O(N log N) time and O(N) heap space. Posting is O(1) amortized, while follow and unfollow are O(1) average. Overall stored space is O(T + F), where T is total tweets and F is total follow links.

There is no need to sort every relevant tweet when the feed can contain only 10 items. Each user's tweet vector is kept in posting order. While visiting the user and all followees, a min-heap stores the best 10 timestamps seen so far.

When an eleventh tweet enters the heap, the oldest one is removed. At the end, popping gives oldest first, so the result is reversed. This code stores all tweets and does not change any input array.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <functional>
#include <queue>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <unordered_set>
#include <utility>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Twitter {
private:
  using Tweet = pair<int, int>;
  unordered_map<int, vector<Tweet>> tweets;
  unordered_map<int, unordered_set<int>> following;
  int time;

public:
  Twitter() : time(0) {
  }

  void postTweet(int userId, int tweetId) {
    tweets[userId].push_back({time++, tweetId});
  }

  vector<int> getNewsFeed(int userId) {
    priority_queue<Tweet, vector<Tweet>, greater<Tweet>> heap;

    auto keepTweet = [&heap](const Tweet& tweet) {
      heap.push(tweet);
      if (heap.size() > 10) {
        heap.pop();
      }
    };

    for (const Tweet& tweet : tweets[userId]) {
      keepTweet(tweet);
    }

    for (int followeeId : following[userId]) {
      for (const Tweet& tweet : tweets[followeeId]) {
        keepTweet(tweet);
      }
    }

    vector<int> feed;
    while (!heap.empty()) {
      feed.push_back(heap.top().second);
      heap.pop();
    }
    reverse(feed.begin(), feed.end());
    return feed;
  }

  void follow(int followerId, int followeeId) {
    if (followerId != followeeId) {
      following[followerId].insert(followeeId);
    }
  }

  void unfollow(int followerId, int followeeId) {
    following[followerId].erase(followeeId);
  }
};
```

If there are fewer than 10 visible tweets, the heap contains only those tweets and all of them are returned. Repeated follow calls are harmless because a set ignores duplicates.

If N is the number of tweets from the user and followees, `getNewsFeed` takes O(N log 10), which is effectively O(N). The heap uses O(10) temporary space. Total stored data takes O(T + F) space.

This keeps the implementation fairly direct. Every tweet gets a counter, and each user's followees are stored in a set. To build a feed, collect tweets from the user and their followees, sort them from newest to oldest, and copy at most 10 IDs.

It is easy to check and works well for the platform limits. The trade-off is that it sorts the complete collected list instead of stopping early.

```go
package main

import "sort"

type tweet struct {
	time   int
	tweetId int
}

type Twitter struct {
	time      int
	tweets    map[int][]tweet
	following map[int]map[int]bool
}

func NewTwitter() *Twitter {
	return &Twitter{
		tweets:    make(map[int][]tweet),
		following: make(map[int]map[int]bool),
	}
}

func (t *Twitter) postTweet(userId int, tweetId int) {
	t.tweets[userId] = append(t.tweets[userId], tweet{
		time:    t.time,
		tweetId: tweetId,
	})
	t.time++
}

func (t *Twitter) getNewsFeed(userId int) []int {
	users := make(map[int]bool)
	users[userId] = true
	for followeeId := range t.following[userId] {
		users[followeeId] = true
	}

	allTweets := make([]tweet, 0)
	for id := range users {
		allTweets = append(allTweets, t.tweets[id]...)
	}

	sort.Slice(allTweets, func(i, j int) bool {
		return allTweets[i].time > allTweets[j].time
	})

	limit := 10
	if len(allTweets) < limit {
		limit = len(allTweets)
	}

	feed := make([]int, 0, limit)
	for i := 0; i < limit; i++ {
		feed = append(feed, allTweets[i].tweetId)
	}
	return feed
}

func (t *Twitter) follow(followerId int, followeeId int) {
	if followerId == followeeId {
		return
	}
	if t.following[followerId] == nil {
		t.following[followerId] = make(map[int]bool)
	}
	t.following[followerId][followeeId] = true
}

func (t *Twitter) unfollow(followerId int, followeeId int) {
	if t.following[followerId] != nil {
		delete(t.following[followerId], followeeId)
	}
}
```

For instance, after tweets with times 0, 1, and 2 are visible, sorting gives 2, 1, 0, and the first 10 entries are copied. An empty tweet list naturally returns an empty slice.

`postTweet`, `follow`, and `unfollow` are O(1) average, apart from sorting costs. If N tweets are visible, `getNewsFeed` takes O(N log N) time and O(N) extra space for the collected slice. All tweets and follow links use O(T + F) stored space.

A single list can keep every tweet in posting order. For the feed, check that list from the newest tweet backward and keep a tweet when its author is the user or someone followed by the user. The scan stops after 10 matches.

Follow relationships are stored in sets, so adding and removing a follow is simple. The list also means the input is not changed, since all data belongs to the Twitter object.

```python
from collections import defaultdict

class Twitter:
    def __init__(self):
        self.time = 0
        self.tweets = []
        self.following = defaultdict(set)

    def postTweet(self, userId, tweetId):
        self.tweets.append((self.time, userId, tweetId))
        self.time += 1

    def getNewsFeed(self, userId):
        visible_users = self.following[userId] | {userId}
        feed = []

        for _, author_id, tweet_id in reversed(self.tweets):
            if author_id in visible_users:
                feed.append(tweet_id)
                if len(feed) == 10:
                    break

        return feed

    def follow(self, followerId, followeeId):
        if followerId != followeeId:
            self.following[followerId].add(followeeId)

    def unfollow(self, followerId, followeeId):
        if followerId != followeeId:
            self.following[followerId].discard(followeeId)
```

For example, if user 1 posts 101, user 2 posts 202, and user 1 follows user 2, the backward scan sees 202 first and then 101, so the feed is `[202, 101]`.

`postTweet`, `follow`, and `unfollow` take O(1) average time. `getNewsFeed` takes O(T) in the worst case, where T is the number of stored tweets. The stored tweets and follow sets use O(T + F) space. An unknown user simply gets an empty feed.
